<h1>Potensic ATOM SE Review: Is It Still Worth Buying in 2026?</h1>
<p><em>By DJI Gear Portal — Affiliate Blog</em></p>

<p>The Potensic ATOM SE is the cheapest drone in Potensic's ATOM family and the one that started the line. It launched in 2022, so it's now two generations behind the ATOM 3. That raises a fair question: is a drone this old still worth buying? Here's what it offers, what it gives up, and who it still suits.</p>

<h2>What You Get</h2>

<p>According to retailer specifications, the ATOM SE shoots 4K video at 30 frames per second with electronic image stabilization. It flies for up to about 31 minutes per battery, and the Fly More Combo includes two 2,500mAh batteries for roughly 62 minutes in total. Transmission range is listed at up to 4 km, and GPS features include Follow Me, Waypoint flight and Circle flight, plus automatic return-to-home if the signal drops or the battery runs low. A deal listing puts its weight at 245g, which keeps it under the 249g threshold that lightens registration in many countries.</p>

<p>When it first appeared, Digital Camera World noted its 4K video beat the 2.7K limit on DJI's cheaper entry drone at the time, the Mini 2 SE.</p>

<h2>What It Gives Up</h2>

<p><strong>No three-axis gimbal.</strong> This is the biggest difference. The ATOM SE relies on electronic stabilization, which crops and processes the image to smooth it. Digital Camera World pointed out that the follow-up model added the mechanical gimbal the SE lacked, and that the gimbal handles shake better than digital stabilization. If smooth video is your priority, that matters.</p>

<p><strong>Shorter range and older tech.</strong> The ATOM raised range from the SE's 4 km to 6 km, and the ATOM 2 and ATOM 3 go further still. The newer models also offer more shooting modes and better cameras.</p>

<p><strong>Limited sensing.</strong> The retailer specs list a downward-facing vision sensor and a time-of-flight sensor, which help with stable low flying and indoor use. They don't list obstacle avoidance, so assume you're responsible for avoiding trees and walls.</p>

<h2>Price</h2>

<p>The SE has been on the market for years, and price varies a lot. A deal listing in 2026 showed it discounted to roughly $137 from a list price near $269, though sale prices like that come and go. Check current pricing before you buy.</p>

<h2>Who Should Buy It</h2>

<p><strong>Buy the ATOM SE if</strong> you want the lowest-cost way to learn to fly a GPS drone with 4K video, you're buying for a child or a first-time pilot who might crash it, or you want a cheap second drone for casual flights.</p>

<p><strong>Skip it if</strong> you care about smooth footage or you plan to create content seriously. For a modest step up in price, the ATOM 2 adds a real gimbal, and the ATOM 3 improves the camera again.</p>

